      "name": "read_query",
      "description": "Dataverse SQL supports only basic SELECT queries with an explicit list of selected fields. \nIf aggregates are used, do not mix aggregated and non-aggregated columns in the SELECT list without a GROUP BY clause. \nIf a query requires comparing to an aggregate (for example, > AVG), do not use subqueries. \nInstead, split the logic into two queries: one to compute the aggregate and another to filter using literal values.\nGETDATE() and GETUTCDATE() functions are supported in predicates but not in the SELECT list.\nThe following features are allowed:\n\nTOP (without OFFSET). Example: SELECT TOP 20 col1 FROM table1\nSimple column-to-literal, column-to-column and column-to-date value function WHERE filters. Example: SELECT age FROM table1 WHERE age > 10 and haircolor=eyecolor or hiredate<GETUTCDATE()\nORDER BY. Example: SELECT col1, age FROM table1 ORDER BY age\nGROUP BY. Only the following aggregates are supported: COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX. Example: SELECT col1, COUNT(col2) AS player_count FROM table1 GROUP BY col1.\nJOINs. Example: SELECT table1.col1, table1.col2, table2.col1 FROM table1 JOIN table2 ON table1.col1 = table2.col1\n\nThe following features are NOT supported:\n\nSubqueries are not allowed in SELECT, WHERE, or FROM clauses.\nHAVING. Example: SELECT col1,COUNT(id) AS player_count FROM table1 GROUP BY col1 HAVING COUNT(id) >= 3\nDISTINCT in select lists. Example: SELECT DISTINCT col1 from table1\nSubqueries (in SELECT, WHERE, FROM). Example: SELECT col1, col2 FROM table1 WHERE col1 IN (SELECT col3 FROM table2)\nWITH keyword. Example: WITH Sales AS (WITH Sales SELECT CxId, SUM(Amt) AS Exp FROM Orders GROUP BY CxId ) SELECT CxId, Exp FROM Sales\nOFFSET keyword. Example: SELECT Id, col1 FROM table1 ORDER BY Id OFFSET\nUNION keyword. Example: SELECT col1, col2 FROM table1 WHERE col3 = 'Sales' UNION SELECT col1, col2 FROM table1 WHERE col3 = 'Marketing'\nCAST keyword. Example: SELECT CAST('123' AS INT) AS ValueInt\nCONVERT keyword. Example: SELECT CONVERT(DATE, OrderDate) AS OrderDateOnly, CONVERT(DECIMAL(10,2), Amt) AS Amount FROM Orders\nCASE keyword. Example: SELECT Id, col1, col2, CASE WHEN Sal >= 100 THEN 'High' WHEN Sal >= 500 THEN 'Medium' ELSE 'Low' END AS SalCat FROM table1\nIF-ELSE keyword. Example: SELECT Id, col1, col2, CASE WHEN Sal > 100 THEN 'High' ELSE 'Low' END AS SalCat FROM table1\nAdvanced functions like: ROUND keyword. Example: SELECT PName, ROUND(Price, 2) AS RoundedPrice FROM table1\nDATE functions. Example: DATEDIFF(), DATEADD(), DATE()\nComplex conditions or unsupported aggregate argument types\nJOINs on aggregates. Example: SELECT c.CxId, c.CxName, s.Exp FROM Cxs c JOIN (SELECT CxId, SUM(Amt) AS Exp FROM Orders GROUP BY CxId) AS s ON c.CxId = s.CxId WHERE s.Exp > 100",
      "inputSchema": {
